## 3. The minimum set, and the cut

**Minimum S11c-b LEDGER (produced bind-set + identity closure + still-live T-substrate / knobs / profiles):**

- `slab_operator` (18.148) + `coupling_kernel` (7.641) + `mu_theta_operator` (0.216)
- T-a..T-i (~0.37)
- knobs / profiles / `q_out` / new `gamma_s11cb_*` free-symbol rows (~0.05)
- optionally, if this file is c1’s **only** import: the tiny S11b B0b/B0c rows c1 §5c binds (`z_impermeable` 402 B, `face_response` 4 KB, …)

**≈ 26.4 MB**, dominated by the two operators c2 folds. The rest of the 61.61 MB is evidence.

**Cut list (this file):**

| cut | MB | % of 61.61 |
|---|---:|---:|
| all `*_term_origins` | 17.87 | 29.0 |
| inherited S11b diagnostics (≥50 KB list above, ~9 MB) + S11 loci (~1.5) | ~10.5 | 17 |
| S11c-a projection package not in T-a..T-i | 1.89 | 3.1 |
| S11c-b `energy_basis_*` + admissibility | 0.95 | 1.5 |
| **subtotal, clearly unbound** | **~31** | **~51** |

After the cut, **~26–28 MB remains**, and **~26 MB of that is `slab_operator` + `coupling_kernel` in expanded form** — a representation problem, not a set problem (§5).

F10 applied only to **c1’s new rows** does **not** shrink this file. `F10:253–254`: “prior committed exports are unchanged.” c1 §7 then **carries the entire inherited LEDGER** (`S11c_c1_SHARED_PHYSICS.md:587–588`; directive `:111–112`). So c1’s export is ≥ 61.61 MB + ~5 MB DtN/response (F10’s own 145 MB measurement: 84.7 MB was two dissipation rows; model-level ~5 MB; the rest is this inherited dump). The 100 MB cap is still in play **because D1’s over-export is being copied forward**, not because c1’s consume-set is large.

S11c-b’s spec §7 **never declares a consume-set**. F10’s fallback (`:239–241`) is exactly why `term_origins` landed in plain git.

---

## 4. Under-export risk, and what actually bounds it

Three failure modes, only one of which is dangerous.

**Dangerous: a construction operand missing from the bind-set.** If `slab_operator` is not in some file c2 imports, c2 cannot fold (`N1`: PY does not re-derive inherited objects). The guard is **not** “export everything.” It is:

- the emitting spec **names** the produced bind-set (union over **known later** steps, not only hop+1);
- that list is physics-bearing ⇒ **two legs before the builder** (rule 7) — this is the same tax F10 already put on the consume-set;
- `D3` round-trip on what **was** written (`S9_REWRITE_PLAN.md:217`);
- “never re-originate” on identity rows (c1 directive `:73`).

**Not dangerous: dropping a diagnostic.** It remains in the annexed `.out` (`F10:244–246`: “Emit-only is never computed then discarded”). Recovery is a promotion into a later consume-set, not a rebuild of physics. The comparator and the review legs never needed the LEDGER (`:248–251`; comparator `:9–10`).

**The next-hop hole, which F10 currently papers over with “carry the whole inherited file.”** c1’s consume-set is DtN + face response (`:347–348, :562–563`). That set does **not** contain `slab_operator`. c2 needs `slab_operator`. Today that works only because c1 copies 61 MB of S11c-b, including 18 MB of operator **and** 17 MB of origins. If we prune S11c-b to **c1’s** consume-set, c2 breaks. The bind-set of S11c-b is `{mu_theta_operator, slab_operator, coupling_kernel}`, which is **larger** than “what the immediate next step binds.”

`D1`’s “errs toward more” (`S9_REWRITE_PLAN.md:210–213`) was the right default when export **was** emit — one channel, no consume-set, no `.out` safety-net. That world is gone. **F10 + the `.out` replace D1’s err-toward-more as a builder default.** What survives from D1 is the one clause that was always the real control: **the builder makes no per-object judgement.** The spec names the bind-set; the builder exports exactly that plus closure.

Unclear object: **emit-only, report it, promote later.** That disagrees with F10’s “the safe direction is still to export” (`:231–232`) and agrees with the c1 directive’s own operational line (`S11c_c1_sympy_build_directive.md:126–128`: unclear → emit-only). The `.out` is the safety-net; exporting a 13 MB origins tree “just in case” is how we got here.

Worst case a step re-derives: true for WL (always; it imports nothing, `S9_REWRITE_PLAN.md:17–18`). False for PY under `N1`. So “just re-derive” is **not** a PY recovery path. Promotion from `.out` is.

---

## 5. Representation vs set (flagged, not solved)

Even the minimum set still contains `slab_operator` at **18.148 MB** (display 4.738 + srepr 13.409, ratio 2.83) and `coupling_kernel` at 7.641 MB (2.004 + 5.636). The prompt’s expansion diagnostic (271 symbols, 365,820 occurrences) is why: the stored object is a flattened sum, not a factored operator.

`D3` (`:217–219`) requires (i) a form SymPy reconstructs exactly and (ii) a human-readable rendering **alongside**. That is why every row stores the expression twice. The comparator canonicalizes by `expand` (`:1206`), so agreement does **not** require the LEDGER to store the expanded representative.

That is a **separate lever** (store unexpanded / drop `display` from the LEDGER and keep it on the `.out` / don’t expand before export). It is not this question. Cutting the set without touching representation still leaves ~26 MB of operators that c2 must bind.

---

## 6. Does this revise F10 or D1 as written?

**D1 — replace, do not “refine.”** `D1` (`S9_REWRITE_PLAN.md:210–213`): *“Export every object S9's MAIN package emits … Mechanical, no per-object judgement, and it errs toward exporting more.”* The no-judgement clause stays. “Every MAIN” and “errs toward more” are the operational default that put `slab_operator_term_origins` (13.1 MB) in plain git. F10 already moved the boundary to the spec-declared consume-set (`:232–236`). Call that a **replacement of D1’s membership rule**, with D1 retained only as the fallback when a spec **has not** declared a bind-set (`F10:239–241`) — and treat a missing consume-set as a **spec defect** (S11c-b §7 is that defect), not as a licence to keep D1 in steady state.

**F10 — revise in three places.**

1. **Membership is the bind test, not the category list.** `F10:224–229` says “model-level register — operators, kernels, closed responses, and the knobs/symbols a later step binds” and then lists diagnostic categories as emit-only. The second half is right. The first half over-generates: `energy_basis_variable` is model-level and unbound; `z_impermeable` is a model object and only a §5c control at c1. Write the bind test; keep the category list as a heuristic the bind test can override.
2. **The consume-set is the produced bind-set (any later step), not hop+1.** c1’s named consume-set (`:347–348`) is correct **for c1→c2**. It is **not** S11c-b’s export set. S11c-b must export `slab_operator` even though c1 is forbidden to import it. F10 as applied to c1, plus “carry the entire inherited LEDGER,” currently hides this by copying 61 MB.
3. **Carried-forward inherited LEDGER means the inherited bind-set, not the inherited file.** `F10:226–227` “merged onto the carried-forward inherited LEDGER” + `:253` “prior committed exports are unchanged” is how 9 MB of S11b growth/decay/causality diagnostics and 17 MB of origins ride into c1. Prune inherited diagnostics at the write. (The 2026-08-11 `chain_accumulate_or_generate_decision.md:8–9` already superseded D5’s physical accumulation — “each step exports only the rows it defines or re-derives” — and was never implemented; F10 of 2026-09-03 re-asserted accumulation. I am not reopening storage topology here. I am saying that **whatever topology is used, membership is the bind-set.**)

`D3` is untouched: round-trip whatever you write; do not write what you do not need to revive.

`F9` is untouched: it decides **keys**, not **which objects exist**.

---

## Disagreement I would expect with F10/D1 as currently written

- F10: “this refines D1, it does not overturn it” and “the safe direction is still to export.” I would overturn D1’s membership default. Unclear → emit-only, promote from `.out`.
- F10: carry the inherited LEDGER whole. I would carry only the inherited bind-set. Otherwise F10 cannot actually shrink the chain.
- F10: emitting spec’s consume-set = what the next step imports. Necessary, not sufficient. S11c-b’s produced bind-set includes objects c1 is forbidden to touch.
- D1-as-fallback on S11c-b (no consume-set) is the measured cause of the 17.9 MB origins blob. That is a spec hole, not a virtue of D1.

---

**Decision-list sentence:**

**The LEDGER carries exactly the bind-set: every object a later chain step looks up by key and uses as a construction operand, plus that set’s free-symbol and `dimension_key` closure; everything else the engine computes — including `*_term_origins`, dissipation/energy/loci/Hermitian/regime views, §5/§6 controls, and named intermediates no later step binds — is emit-only in the annexed `.out`.**
